# Leverage and Systemic Risk

Leverage can amplify gains but also lead to devastating losses. When excessive leverage is employed without proper skin in the game, it increases systemic risk and can cause financial crises. Taleb uses historical examples like the Great Depression and the subprime mortgage crisis to illustrate how decision-makers who bear no personal downside can take on catastrophic risks at the expense of others.

The core insight is that systems without accountability—where those making leveraged bets face no consequences—produce moral hazard and fragility. Having skin in the game ensures that risk-takers internalize the downside of their decisions, creating a natural check against excessive risk-taking.

This concept connects to [[antifragility]] in that antifragile systems benefit from stressors, while over-leveraged systems without accountability are inherently fragile and prone to catastrophic failure.

## Key Points

- Excessive leverage without skin in the game increases systemic risk
- Historical examples: Great Depression, subprime mortgage crisis
- Moral hazard arises when decision-makers face no downside
- Accountability aligns incentives and reduces catastrophic risk
- [[skin-in-the-game]] is the mechanism that prevents systemic fragility

